  "question": {
    "text": "The combustion of sodium in excess air yields a higher oxide. What is the oxidation state of the oxygen in the product? Neglect the negative sign.",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [],
  "correctValue": "1",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>  </mtext><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi>Na</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p><p>It exists as&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>Na</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo></mrow></msubsup></math>&nbsp;</p><p>So oxidation state of O is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
